Client server model :- 
client server model is a model between your personal computer which acts as a client and a server which is responsible for providing data to a client
think of it as an example you want to watch youtube , you enter youtube.com on your browswer now here your browser is a client which is requesting to access
youtube webpage now this request will be carried to the youtube's server which will grant you the access to the webpage.

Protocol - a protocol defines how a client can communicate to a server , it is a set of rules which need to be followed during networking because protocol is what
ensures the data is being served completely and securly 

Port - A port is used to identify a specific service running on the system , when a client has to access a server it must be connceted to a right port

Domain name service (DNS) - It is a system that translates website names (domain names) into IP addresses that computers use to communicate.

HTTP/HTTPS is stateless → each request is independent; the server doesn't inherently remember previous requests.
Sessions add state → websites use mechanisms like session IDs to remember that you're logged in.
The session ID is commonly stored in a cookie or token and sent with subsequent requests.
Without sessions, you'd have to authenticate on every request.
